/// Which screen is showing.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum Screen {
    /// Save files found on the SD card.
    SaveList,
    /// Character slot picker.
    Slots,
    /// Scalar character fields.
    Char,
    /// Item box.
    Items,
    /// Quest .arc files found on the SD card.
    ArcList,
    /// Download quests stored inside the open save.
    SaveQuests,
    /// Entries inside the open .arc.
    ArcEntries,
    /// .mib field editor.
    Mib,
    /// .gmd text viewer / editor.
    Gmd,
    /// Quest checker results.
    Check,
    /// Charm box (護石).
    Charms,
    /// Charm editor form.
    CharmEdit,
    /// Decoded view of one .rem / .sup / .sem / .esl / .qdp / .qdl entry.
    EntryView,
    /// Skill name picker for the talisman editor.
    SkillPick,
    /// 3DS <-> Switch conversion for the open save.
    Convert,
    /// The equipment box of the current character.
    Equip,
    /// The equipment editor form (level, transmog, decorations).
    EquipEdit,
    /// Decoration name picker, opened from the equipment form with Y.
    JewelPick,
    /// Equipment name picker, opened from the form's 種別 / ID row with Y.
    ///
    /// The box's ID row used to be a number the user stepped with the arrow
    /// keys, which is 1,606 weapons and 6,435 armour pieces of blind hunting.
    /// This screen lists the game's own names for the half of the box the form
    /// is on, narrows them by type and by a typed word, and writes the type and
    /// the id back into the form with [A].
    EquipPick,
    /// The 45 quest-equipment entries the DLC section carries.
    QuestEquip,
    /// Monster hunt, capture and size records.
    Hunts,
    /// The guild card and the arena log.
    Guild,
    /// The palico roster.
    Roster,
    /// Item sets and equipment sets.
    Sets,
    /// Item name search, opened from the item box with R.
    ItemPick,
    /// Download Palicoes (配信オトモ).
    Palico,
    /// Global options.
    Options,
    /// CSV export and import of the item box and the equipment box.
    ///
    /// A screen of its own rather than a mode: the folder it lists has nothing
    /// to do with the record that is open, and the two keys that matter (write
    /// the boxes out, apply a file) are the whole screen.
    Csv,
    /// Per-slot progress: guild card awards, request flags and quest bits.
    ///
    /// One screen with three views (勲章 / 依頼 / クエスト) rather than three
    /// screens: they are the same question - what has this character done -
    /// about three bit maps that all sit inside the character slot, and they
    /// share the same two keys (a row, and one bit on that row).
    ///
    /// Every action here flips **one bit per press**. Nothing on this screen
    /// takes a second press to confirm, because nothing here can lose a record:
    /// an award or a quest bit is a single flag the game itself re-merges, so
    /// pressing the same key again is the undo.
    Progress,
    /// クエスト改造 - the quest as one picture, not as fifteen files.
    ///
    /// A quest archive is fifteen entries: `.mib` parameters, `.gmd` text,
    /// up to five `.rem` reward tables, a `.sup` supply box and four `.sem`
    /// placements. Asking the user to pick one of them flipped a coin every
    /// time: the number that needed changing lived in a different file from the
    /// row that named it. This screen is the coin landing once - the game's own
    /// four questions (基本 / モンスター / 報酬 / 支給品) over the same bytes -
    /// and [Screen::ArcEntries] stays one press away behind 詳細.
    ///
    /// Nothing here is a new offset. Every row is read and written through the
    /// writer the file screen already uses: the .mib fields through
    /// [Editor::step_mib_field], the reward rows through the .rem table writer,
    /// and the placements through [Editor::adjust_sem_placement].
    QuestEdit,
    /// 生データ (セーブマップ) - the bytes behind the screen the user came from.
    ///
    /// The 詳細 button on every record screen lands here, on the row
    /// [crate::savemap] names for the region that screen edits (装備ボックス,
    /// 護石, the item box, the award / request / quest bit maps …), and the
    /// right-hand panel is that row's hex dump. It is the binary view the
    /// reference editor calls セーブマップ.
    ///
    /// The screen is a viewer **and** an editor. The list moves a cursor and
    /// the dump shows the selected row's bytes; [A] opens the byte editor on
    /// that row ([Editor::map_edit_enter]), where one byte at a time may be
    /// rewritten after the status line spells the change out and a second [A]
    /// confirms it. Every confirmed change is one whole-save undo step, the
    /// caret cannot leave the row, and a structural byte (the body header, a
    /// pointer, a record's size/id pair, a save-screen copy) is warned about
    /// rather than refused.
    ///
    /// [B] goes back to the screen 詳細 was pressed on, which is why the screen
    /// keeps the name of that screen rather than a stack.
    SaveMap,
    /// 変更一覧 - every value that differs from the file on disk, one line
    /// each, with the one-item undo the pre-save review does not offer.
    ///
    /// The same walk the pre-save dialog runs ([crate::diffvalues]) over the
    /// save that is open and the bytes the card holds, so the two screens can
    /// never name a change differently. The reference is the file as it was
    /// last read or written ([Editor::set_review_reference]); a line is put back
    /// by copying the bytes that line owns out of that reference, and the copy
    /// is checked against the whole value walk before it is accepted - see
    /// [plan_review_rows], which is the same rule the desktop review follows.
    ///
    /// Nothing here writes a file: the open save changes, the save key puts it
    /// out, and the step goes onto the same LIFO history as every other edit.
    Review,
}

/// Which press asked the "drop this form's draft" question.
///
/// One flag used to serve every route, so a question asked by a jump was spent
/// by the next [B] - and a [B] offer was spent by the next shoulder press, which
/// dropped the draft on the *first* press of a key that had asked nothing. The
/// route that asked is part of the answer now: only that key can spend it.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
enum FormLeaveAsk {
    /// Nothing is waiting.
    None,
    /// [B] on the form.
    Back,
    /// A sub-tab or section jump.
    Jump,
    /// A slot switch.
    Slot,
}

/// What a shoulder press means on the screen that is showing.
///
/// [L] / [R] are the sub-tab keys, and that is the whole navigation rule: a
/// shoulder press moves the tab strip and nothing else. The rule is one line
/// because the two keys are one band - the console answers them on the same
/// frame, and a screen that took [R] back made one gesture mean two things
/// depending on which shoulder was used. That is exactly what the pad did: [L]
/// moved the tab and [R] opened a keyboard on the item box, the hunt record,
/// the guild card, the reward rows and the equipment form, so a user crossing
/// the hunt record's tabs with the shoulders landed in the number entry and
/// could not reach the guild card at all.
///
/// The jobs [R] carried are [ZR] held now ([Editor::zr_hold_job]), which is the
/// key those screens already own for their second action - so nothing lost a
/// key, and no screen can take a shoulder back again.
///
/// Three answers, and every one of them is named here rather than left to a
/// negative list at the call site.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum Shoulder {
    /// Previous / next sub-tab: the answer on every screen of the editor.
    SubTab,
    /// The ten-row jump of a pick list: the shoulder is turned into [◀] / [▶].
    Move,
    /// A modal panel that owns every key while it is open (the palico skill
    /// panel): the press is forwarded to the panel, and the tab strip under it
    /// must not move. No *screen* answers this - a screen that did would be
    /// taking a shoulder back, which is the bug this enum exists to make
    /// impossible.
    Panel,
    /// Nobody: a modal form owns the screen and neither reads the shoulder nor
    /// lets the tab strip under it move.
    Swallow,
}

/// What [ZR] means on the screen that is showing.
///
/// The review found two problems with one cause. The progress screen's search
/// was drawn as "[検索] 名前" and answered `Key::Z` - but the console spends ZR
/// on the section bar and then dropped the key before the state machine saw it,
/// so the one key the guide named could not be pressed. And [-] carried nine
/// meanings, one of which was 削除: a key that deletes on one screen and opens a
/// menu on the next is a key nobody can learn.
///
/// So the two are the same answer. [-] is the delete key and nothing else, and
/// the jobs it used to do are [ZR]'s - which means ZR has to become a key the
/// console actually delivers. [Editor::zr_job] is that question, asked once, so
/// the console's dispatch, the footer and the host tests cannot disagree about
/// whether this screen's ZR is the section bar or the screen's own second
/// action.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum ZrJob {
    /// The section bar: the console cycles it and the state machine never sees
    /// the key. This is the default, and it is what the header's ZR button
    /// promises.
    Sidebar,
    /// The screen's own second action. The console hands the press to the state
    /// machine, exactly as it does for every other key.
    Screen,
}

/// What a *held* [ZR] means on the screen that is showing.
///
/// The jobs [R] carried before the shoulders became one band live here now: the
/// number keyboard of the item box, the hunt record, the guild card and the
/// reward / supply rows, the item search of the quest editor's two item tabs,
/// the 既読 bit of the progress screen's quest view, the id keyboard of the
/// equipment form and ポーチへ適用 on the item set form. [R] is a tab key on
/// every one of those screens now, so each job needed a key of its own - and
/// [ZR] is the key those screens already own (its tap is the screen's second
/// action, [Editor::zr_job]), so the hold is the second job of the same key
/// rather than a new key nobody has.
///
/// The hold is answered on the frame it crosses the threshold, the same way
/// [R]'s 詳細 is ([ShoulderTap]): a press that lingers is the hold, and a press
/// that comes up first is still the tap.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum ZrHold {
    /// No second job behind the key on this screen: the hold does nothing and
    /// the press is still the tap.
    None,
    /// The job [R] used to carry. The console runs [Editor::zr_hold_press].
    Action,
}

/// What 詳細 would open, captured when the key went down.
///
/// [R]'s tap waits for the release (see [ShoulderTap]), and the hold opens the
/// byte view of the screen the user was on when they pressed. Three screens
/// resolve that row from a view that lives in the console layer - マイセット's
/// two arrays, 進行状況's three bit maps, the card's four views - so the view
/// numbers travel with the screen rather than being read again on the release
/// frame.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct DetailTarget {
    /// The screen 詳細 was pressed on.
    pub screen: Screen,
    /// [Editor::progress_view] at that moment.
    pub progress_view: usize,
    /// [Editor::guild_view] at that moment.
    pub guild_view: usize,
    /// The console's マイセット tab (0 = items, 1 = equipment).
    pub sets_tab: usize,
}

